Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>8  Swapping data</H1>
<HR>
An address book shouldn't be an island. Other programs - the address books in phones, email programs,
spreadsheets - can swap contacts using two common formats, vCard and CSV. In this chapter Contacts learns to
read and write both: you'll be able to drop other programs' files on it, save contacts for other programs,
and drag contacts straight out of the list. The program is <CODE>!Contacts8</CODE>, and the new code is in the
toolkit module <CODE>Formats</CODE> and a module of Contacts' own, <CODE>Import</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Two formats</H2>
A <I>vCard</I> is the standard way to pass on one person's details; a <CODE>.vcf</CODE> file holds one or
more of them. Each card is lines of text between <CODE>BEGIN:VCARD</CODE> and <CODE>END:VCARD</CODE>, each
line a property's name, a colon and its value:<P>
<PRE>
BEGIN:VCARD
VERSION:3.0
N:Smith;Ann;;Dr;
FN:Ann Smith
TEL;TYPE=CELL:07700 900123
EMAIL;TYPE=INTERNET:ann.smith@example.com
ADR;TYPE=HOME:;;1 Acorn Road\nCambridge;;;CB1 2AB;
END:VCARD
</PRE>
Some properties have several parts separated by semicolons: <CODE>N</CODE> is surname; first name; other
names; title; suffix, and <CODE>ADR</CODE> has seven parts (post office box; extended address; street;
town; county or region; postcode; country). Contacts writes its address into the street part and the
postcode into the postcode part; reading, it puts the street, town, region and country together, a line
each, as the address. Things after a semicolon in the name (<CODE>TYPE=CELL</CODE>) are <I>parameters</I>, saying what
kind of telephone number or address it is. A newline in a value is written <CODE>\n</CODE>, and a
semicolon or comma that is part of a value has a <CODE>\</CODE> in front. Contacts writes version 3.0 and
reads 3.0 and the older 2.1, which many programs still write.<P>
<I>CSV</I> (comma-separated values) is simpler: a table as text, a line for each row with the values
separated by commas. The first row usually holds the columns' titles:<P>
<PRE>
First name,Surname,Mobile,Email
Ann,Smith,07700 900123,ann.smith@example.com
"Bob, Jr",Jones,,bob@acornpies.example
</PRE>
A value containing a comma, a quote or a newline is put in double quotes, and a quote inside it is written
twice. Every spreadsheet reads and writes CSV, which makes it the easy way to get a list of names into and
out of other programs.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Characters</H2>
There is a snag. Text is stored as numbers, one for each character, and different systems number characters
differently. RISC OS 3.71 uses <I>Latin-1</I>: 256 characters, one byte each, enough for English and western
European languages (&pound;, &eacute;, &szlig;...). Today's programs use <I>Unicode</I>, which has room for
every alphabet (and emoji), usually stored as <I>UTF-8</I>: the first 128 characters are one byte each, the
same as Latin-1, but everything else takes two to four bytes. So a UTF-8 file read as if it were Latin-1
shows &eacute; as two strange characters, and a Latin-1 file read as UTF-8 goes wrong the other way.<P>
<CODE>Formats</CODE> has two functions to deal with this:<P>
<PRE>
// ---------------------------------------------------- text and bytes
export function decode(bytes) {
  let text;
  try {
    text = new TextDecoder('utf-8', { fatal: true }).decode(bytes);
  } catch {                           // not UTF-8: take it as Latin-1
    return Array.from(bytes, (b) =&gt; String.fromCharCode(b)).join('');
  }
  // RISC OS text is Latin-1: other characters become '?'
  return text.replace(/[^\x00-\xff]/g, '?');
}
export const encode = (text) =&gt; new TextEncoder().encode(text);
</PRE>
<CODE>decode</CODE> turns the bytes of a file into text. It tries UTF-8 first - <CODE>TextDecoder</CODE>
with <CODE>fatal: true</CODE> gives up with an error if the bytes aren't valid UTF-8 - and if that fails,
takes each byte as a Latin-1 character. (<CODE>catch {</CODE> with no name in brackets is allowed when the
error itself isn't needed. <CODE>Array.from(bytes, fn)</CODE> makes an array from something list-like - here
the bytes - passing each through <CODE>fn</CODE> on the way; <CODE>String.fromCharCode(b)</CODE> is the
character with code <CODE>b</CODE>.) Any character outside Latin-1 becomes a <CODE>?</CODE>, because
RISC OS couldn't show it. <CODE>encode</CODE> turns text into UTF-8 bytes for the files Contacts writes for
other programs, which will expect UTF-8.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Writing vCards</H2>
<PRE>
// ---------------------------------------------------- vCard
const esc = (s) =&gt; String(s).replace(/\\/g, '\\\\')
  .replace(/\n/g, '\\n').replace(/([,;])/g, '\\$1');
const unesc = (s) =&gt; s.replace(/\\(.)/g,
  (_, c) =&gt; (c === 'n' || c === 'N' ? '\n' : c));
// split on ; (or ,) but not on \; (or \,)
const parts = (s, sep) =&gt; s.split(new RegExp(`(?&lt;!\\\\)${sep}`));

export function vcardWrite(contacts) {
  const out = [];
  for (const c of contacts) {
    const lines = ['BEGIN:VCARD', 'VERSION:3.0',
      `N:${esc(c.last ?? '')};${esc(c.first ?? '')};;` +
        `${esc(c.title ?? '')};`,
      `FN:${esc(fullName(c))}`];
    const add = (name, value) =&gt; {
      if (value) lines.push(name + value);
    };
    add('ORG:', c.company &amp;&amp; esc(c.company));
    add('TITLE:', c.job &amp;&amp; esc(c.job));
    add('TEL;TYPE=WORK,VOICE:', c.work);
    add('TEL;TYPE=HOME,VOICE:', c.home);
    add('TEL;TYPE=CELL:', c.mobile);
    add('EMAIL;TYPE=INTERNET:', c.email);
    if (c.address || c.postcode) {
      lines.push(`ADR;TYPE=HOME:;;${esc(c.address ?? '')};;;` +
        `${esc(c.postcode ?? '')};`);
    }
    add('URL:', c.web);
    add('BDAY:', c.birthday);
    add('NOTE:', c.notes &amp;&amp; esc(c.notes));
    add('CATEGORIES:', c.category &amp;&amp; esc(c.category));
    lines.push('END:VCARD');
    out.push(...lines.map(fold));
  }
  return out.join('\r\n') + '\r\n';
}
// A long line is folded: a line break, then a space starts more. No
// line may be over 75 octets as the file is written - in UTF-8
// (encode), where characters above 127 take two.
export function fold(line) {
  let out = '', n = 0;
  for (const ch of line) {
    const w = ch.charCodeAt(0) &gt; 127 ? 2 : 1;
    if (n + w &gt; 75) { out += '\r\n '; n = 1; }
    out += ch; n += w;
  }
  return out;
}
</PRE>
<CODE>esc</CODE> puts a <CODE>\</CODE> before backslashes, commas and semicolons and writes newlines as
<CODE>\n</CODE>. <CODE>vcardWrite</CODE> builds each card as an array of lines. The little function
<CODE>add</CODE> only adds a property if the contact has a value for it, so there are no empty lines.<P>
Lines are joined with <CODE>\r\n</CODE> (carriage return and newline), as the vCard standard asks, and a line
longer than the standard allows is <I>folded</I>: broken, with a space at the start of the next line. The
limit is 75 <I>octets</I> - bytes - as the file is written, and Contacts writes UTF-8, where a character above
127 (such as &eacute;) takes two bytes. So <CODE>fold</CODE>, which is exported because the organiser's
calendar files use the same rule (chapter 16), goes through the line a character at a time with
<CODE>for...of</CODE>, counting one or two for each, and starts a new line (with its space) before the count
would pass 75.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Reading vCards</H2>
Reading is the harder half, because other programs write vCards in many small variations.<P>
<PRE>
export function vcardRead(text) {
  // unfold, and join vCard 2.1's quoted-printable values, which end a
  // line with = to go on (only those: other values may end with =)
  const lines = [];
  for (const l of text.replace(/\r?\n[ \t]/g, '').split(/\r?\n/)) {
    const last = lines.at(-1);
    if (last?.endsWith('=') &amp;&amp; /QUOTED-PRINTABLE/i
      .test(last.slice(0, last.indexOf(':')))) {
      lines[lines.length - 1] = last.slice(0, -1) + l;
    } else lines.push(l);
  }
  const cards = [];
  let c = null;
  for (const line of lines) {
    const m = /^([^:;]+)([^:]*):(.*)$/.exec(line);
    if (!m) continue;
    const name = m[1].toUpperCase().replace(/^.*\./, '');
    const params = m[2].toUpperCase();
    let value = m[3];
    if (/QUOTED-PRINTABLE/.test(params)) {
      // =XX is a byte; in UTF-8 if the value says so
      const bytes = value.replace(/=([0-9A-F]{2})/gi,
        (_, h) =&gt; String.fromCharCode(parseInt(h, 16)));
      value = /CHARSET=UTF-8/.test(params)
        ? decode(Uint8Array.from(bytes, (ch) =&gt; ch.charCodeAt(0)))
        : bytes;
    }
    if (name === 'BEGIN') { c = {}; continue; }
    if (!c) continue;
    const type = (t) =&gt; new RegExp(`\\b${t}\\b`).test(params);
    const v = unesc(value);
</PRE>
First the folded lines are joined up again. vCard 2.1's <I>quoted-printable</I> values have line breaks of
their own - a line ending in <CODE>=</CODE> goes on on the next - so a line ending in <CODE>=</CODE> is joined
to the next, but only if its parameters say <CODE>QUOTED-PRINTABLE</CODE>: other values, such as a picture
coded as text, may end in <CODE>=</CODE> quite properly, and joining those would swallow the card's
<CODE>END</CODE>. Then each line is split by a regular expression into the property's
name (<CODE>m[1]</CODE>), its parameters (<CODE>m[2]</CODE>) and its value (<CODE>m[3]</CODE>). Some
programs put a group name before the property (<CODE>item1.EMAIL</CODE>); <CODE>replace(/^.*\./, '')</CODE>
removes it. Quoted-printable values have bytes written as <CODE>=</CODE> and two hex digits
(<CODE>=E9</CODE> is &eacute; in Latin-1). Those are turned back into bytes, and if the parameters say
<CODE>CHARSET=UTF-8</CODE>, the bytes are decoded as UTF-8 with <CODE>decode</CODE>
(<CODE>Uint8Array.from</CODE> makes the array of bytes it needs), because in UTF-8 &eacute; is two bytes,
<CODE>=C3=A9</CODE>.<P>
<PRE>
    switch (name) {
      case 'N': {
        const [last, first, , title] = parts(value, ';').map(unesc);
        if (last) c.last = last;
        if (first) c.first = first;
        if (title) c.title = title;
        break;
      }
      case 'FN': c.fn = v; break;
      case 'ORG': c.company = unesc(parts(value, ';')[0]); break;
      case 'TITLE': c.job = v; break;
      case 'TEL':
        if (type('CELL')) c.mobile ??= v;
        else if (type('HOME')) c.home ??= v;
        else if (!type('FAX')) c.work ??= v;
        break;
      case 'EMAIL': c.email ??= v; break;
      case 'ADR': {
        const a = parts(value, ';').map(unesc);
        c.address ??= [a[2], a[3], a[4], a[6]].filter(Boolean)
          .join('\n');
        c.postcode ??= a[5];
        break;
      }
      case 'URL': c.web = v; break;
      case 'BDAY': c.birthday = v.replace(/^(\d{4})(\d\d)(\d\d)$/,
        '$1-$2-$3').slice(0, 10); break;
      case 'NOTE': c.notes = v; break;
      case 'CATEGORIES':
        c.category = unesc(parts(value, ',')[0]);
        break;
      case 'END':
        if (!c.first &amp;&amp; !c.last &amp;&amp; c.fn) c.first = c.fn;
        delete c.fn;
        cards.push(c);
        c = null;
        break;
      default: break;
    }
  }
  return cards;
}
</PRE>
A <CODE>switch</CODE> then puts each property's value into the contact being read. <CODE>c.mobile ??= v</CODE>
(chapter 3) sets it only if it isn't set already: a card may have several mobile numbers, and Contacts keeps
the first. <CODE>const [last, first, , title] = ...</CODE> is array destructuring, as in chapter 3's
<CODE>dateKey</CODE>; the empty place between the commas skips the third part.<P>
<CODE>parts</CODE> splits a value at its semicolons - but not at <CODE>\;</CODE>, a semicolon that is part
of the value. That's what <CODE>(?&lt;!\\)</CODE> in its regular expression means: "not after a
backslash".<P>
At <CODE>END</CODE>, a card with no name parts but a full name (<CODE>FN</CODE>) uses that as the first
name, and the contact is added to the list.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>CSV</H2>
<PRE>
// ---------------------------------------------------- CSV
const quote = (s) =&gt; (/[&quot;,\r\n]/.test(s)
  ? `&quot;${s.replace(/&quot;/g, '&quot;&quot;')}&quot;` : s);

export function csvWrite(contacts) {
  const rows = [FIELDS.map(([, title]) =&gt; title)];
  for (const c of contacts) {
    rows.push(FIELDS.map(([k]) =&gt; c[k] ?? ''));
  }
  return rows.map((r) =&gt; r.map((s) =&gt; quote(String(s))).join(','))
    .join('\r\n') + '\r\n';
}

/** The rows of a CSV file: an array of arrays of strings. */
export function csvRows(text) {
  const rows = [[]];
  let field = '', inQuotes = false;
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; text.length; i++) {
    const ch = text[i];
    if (inQuotes) {
      if (ch === '&quot;' &amp;&amp; text[i + 1] === '&quot;') { field += '&quot;'; i++; }
      else if (ch === '&quot;') inQuotes = false;
      else field += ch;
    } else if (ch === '&quot;') inQuotes = true;
    else if (ch === ',') { rows.at(-1).push(field); field = ''; }
    else if (ch === '\n' || ch === '\r') {
      if (ch === '\r' &amp;&amp; text[i + 1] === '\n') i++;
      rows.at(-1).push(field); field = ''; rows.push([]);
    } else field += ch;
  }
  if (field || rows.at(-1).length) rows.at(-1).push(field);
  return rows.filter((r) =&gt; r.some((s) =&gt; s !== ''));
}

export function csvRead(text) {
  const [header, ...rows] = csvRows(text);
  if (!header) return [];
  // match each column to a field by its title (or the field's name)
  const key = (t) =&gt; t.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z]/g, '');
  const cols = header.map((t) =&gt; FIELDS.find(([k, title]) =&gt;
    key(title) === key(t) || k === key(t))?.[0]);
  return rows.map((r) =&gt; {
    const c = {};
    r.forEach((s, i) =&gt; { if (cols[i] &amp;&amp; s) c[cols[i]] = s; });
    return c;
  });
}
</PRE>
<CODE>csvWrite</CODE> makes the header row from the fields' titles, then a row per contact, quoting the
values that need it. <CODE>csvRows</CODE> reads a CSV file a character at a time, because a quoted value can
contain commas and even newlines, so splitting at every comma or line break would go wrong. It remembers
whether it is inside quotes: there, a doubled quote is one quote and a single quote ends the value; outside,
a comma ends a value and a newline ends a row. <CODE>rows.at(-1)</CODE> is the last row (chapter 3).<P>
<CODE>csvRead</CODE> starts with <CODE>const [header, ...rows] = csvRows(text)</CODE>: destructuring with a
<I>rest</I> - <CODE>...rows</CODE> takes everything after the first item as an array of its own, so
<CODE>header</CODE> is the first row and <CODE>rows</CODE> the others. It matches each column to a field by its title, ignoring case, spaces and punctuation, so
<CODE>Surname</CODE>, <CODE>surname</CODE> and <CODE>SURNAME</CODE> all find the surname. Columns it doesn't
know are left out.<P>
All of these are ordinary functions of text, with nothing to do with windows, so Contacts' Check program
tests them: it writes a card and a CSV file and reads them back, reads a vCard 2.1 card and a CSV value with
quotes and a comma in it, and decodes UTF-8 text containing a character RISC OS doesn't have.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Files dropped on Contacts</H2>
Contacts' own module <CODE>Import</CODE> uses <CODE>Formats</CODE> to add other programs' contacts to an
address book:<P>
<PRE>
// Import - contacts from other programs' files (vCard or CSV) added
// to an address book.
//
//   await importFiles(doc, files)   files dropped on a window or icon
//   importData(doc, bytes)          data from another program
import { vfs, reportError } from 'riscos';
import { decode, vcardRead, csvRead } from './Toolkit/Formats';

export async function importFiles(doc, files) {
  for (const f of files) {
    if (f.type === 'dir') continue;
    if (f.filetype === doc.app.TYPE) {     // an address book: open it
      await doc.app.open(f.path);
      continue;
    }
    importData(doc, await vfs.readFile(f.path));
  }
}

/** Add the contacts found in bytes; returns how many there were. */
export function importData(doc, bytes) {
  const text = decode(bytes);
  const cards = /BEGIN:VCARD/i.test(text) ? vcardRead(text)
    : csvRead(text);
  if (!cards.length) {
    reportError('There are no contacts in that file');
    return 0;
  }
  for (const c of cards) doc.book.add(c);
  return cards.length;
}
</PRE>
<CODE>importData</CODE> decodes the bytes, and reads them as vCards if they contain
<CODE>BEGIN:VCARD</CODE>, otherwise as CSV. <CODE>importFiles</CODE> deals with a list of dropped files: a
Contacts file is opened as an address book of its own, and anything else is imported.<P>
When files are dropped on a window, the window gets a <CODE>'dataload'</CODE> event listing them, as Doodle
did in the first book. Contacts' window also listens for <CODE>'datasave'</CODE>, which is what happens when another
program's Save box icon is dropped on it - Edit's, say:<P>
<PRE>
    w.on('dataload', (ev) =&gt; {        // files dropped on the window
      importFiles(doc, ev.files);
      return true;
    });
    w.on('datasave', async (ev) =&gt; {       // another program's data
      importData(doc, await ev.receive());
      return true;
    });
</PRE>
Then there is no file: the data comes straight from the other program. <CODE>await ev.receive()</CODE> asks
for it and gives back its bytes.<P>
Files dropped on the icon bar icon make a new address book to hold what they bring:<P>
<PRE>
    onDataLoad: async (ev) =&gt; {
      for (const f of ev.files) {
        if (f.filetype === TYPE) await app.open(f.path);
        else await importFiles(new Document(app), [f]);
      }
    },
</PRE>
