<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Saving for other programs</H2>
<I>File &gt; Export</I> has two submenus, <I>vCard</I> and <I>CSV</I>, each a Save box. The Style Guide's
rule is that Save saves in the application's own format, and other formats are <I>exported</I>:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ other programs
  /** The contacts to export: the chosen ones, or all of them. */
  chosen() {
    const sel = this.list.selected();
    return sel.length ? sel : this.list.items;
  }
  exportBox(kind) {
    const vcard = kind === 'vcard';
    return saveAs({
      task: this.app.task, filetype: vcard ? 0xFFF : 0xDFE,
      filename: vcard ? 'Cards/vcf' : 'Contacts/csv',
      getData: async () =&gt; encode(vcard ? vcardWrite(this.chosen())
        : csvWrite(this.chosen())),
    });
  }
</PRE>
It exports the chosen contacts, or, if none is chosen, all the contacts the list is showing - so with
something typed in the Find field, only the ones that match. The vCard file is given type Text, as
vCard has no RISC OS type of its own, and the CSV file type &amp;DFE, which is CSV's. The names end
<CODE>/vcf</CODE> and <CODE>/csv</CODE>: RISC OS writes a <CODE>.</CODE> in a file name as <CODE>/</CODE>,
so on a computer that uses extensions (or through HostFS) they become <CODE>Cards.vcf</CODE> and
<CODE>Contacts.csv</CODE>, which other programs recognise.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/C8Export" WIDTH=1024 HEIGHT=768></CENTER>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Dragging contacts out</H2>
The most RISC OS way to pass contacts on is to drag them: choose some, and drag them from the list to a
directory display - or into another program's window. <CODE>ListView</CODE> emits a <CODE>'drag'</CODE>
event when a drag starts on the chosen rows, and the window passes it on:<P>
<PRE>
  dragOut(ev) {
    const sel = this.list.selected();
    const leaf = sel.length === 1
      ? nameOf(sel[0]).replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9]+/g, '_') : 'Cards';
    dragSave(ev, { task: this.app.task, leafname: leaf + '/vcf',
      filetype: 0xFFF,
      getData: async () =&gt; encode(vcardWrite(sel)) });
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>dragSave</CODE>, which the desktop provides, does the rest: it drags a file icon, and when you let go
it writes the data into the directory display it's dropped on, or offers it to the program whose window it
is. The file is named after the contact (with anything that isn't a letter or digit turned into
<CODE>_</CODE>, which a file name can hold), or <CODE>Cards</CODE> for several.<P>

